{% block title %}How it works · {{ cfg.site_title }}{% endblock %}
{% block description %}What SB 63's financial efficiency review is, what the transit agencies have to do, and how their regional tax funding depends on it.{% endblock %}
{% block main %}
<span class="rt-eyebrow">Background</span>
<h1>How SB 63's efficiency requirements work</h1>
<p class="rt-lede">The state law that created the Bay Area's 2026 regional transit tax also requires four of the
  largest transit agencies to look for savings. Their share of the tax money depends on following through.</p>

<div class="rt-box">
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The tax.</strong> SB 63 authorized a 14-year sales tax for transit: 0.5% in Alameda, Contra
      Costa, San Mateo, and Santa Clara counties, and 1% in San Francisco, subject to voter approval on
      November 3, 2026. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67730(c)", 6) }}</span></li>
    <li><strong>The review.</strong> MTC, the regional transportation agency, had to hire an outside consultant
      to review the finances of AC Transit, BART, Caltrain, and Muni (SFMTA) in two phases.
      <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67762", 12) }}, {{ law("67764", 13) }}</span></li>
    <li><strong>The commitments.</strong> After Phase 1, each agency had to adopt the strategies it commits to.
      If the tax passes, Phase 2 follows, and each agency must adopt and follow an implementation plan.
      <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766", 14) }}</span></li>
    <li><strong>The consequence.</strong> An agency that is not in compliance can have its tax funds withheld,
      and eventually given to other transit in the same counties. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(h)", 15) }}</span></li>
  </ul>
</div>

{% with compact=false, link_base="" %}{% include "_steps.html.j2" %}{% endwith %}

<section class="rt-section" id="review">
  <h2>1. The independent review, Phase 1</h2>
  <p><strong>Who did it.</strong> MTC hired the consulting firm Nelson\Nygaard. The law requires the consultant
    to work with the agencies and with an oversight committee made up of an MTC commissioner, a board member from
    each of the four agencies, four independent experts in transit operations and finance appointed by MTC, and
    nonvoting representatives of the state Transportation Agency and Department of Finance.
    <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67764", "13–14") }}; {{ rpt(1) }}</span></p>
  <p><strong>What it covered.</strong> The law limits Phase 1 to three things: cost-saving measures the agencies
    had already taken since 2020, “early action strategies” that would help them deliver more or better service
    and a better rider experience with existing resources, and an inventory of each agency's real estate.
    <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67762(b)", "12–13") }}</span></p>
  <p><strong>What it found about past savings.</strong> For fiscal years 2020 to 2025, the report says AC Transit
    saved almost $200 million in operating costs (7%), BART $516 million (9%), Caltrain more than $76 million
    (7%), and SFMTA nearly $300 million (4%). <span class="rt-cite">{{ rpt(3) }}</span></p>
  <p><strong>What an early action strategy is.</strong> The report describes them as actions agencies “can
    implement or study in the near term, within the next one to three years.” Many are studies or plans rather
    than changes that save money by themselves. Phase 1 deliberately left out strategies that would need new
    upfront revenue, major service changes, changes to labor agreements, long-term restructuring, or new legal
    authority; those are reserved for Phase 2. <span class="rt-cite">{{ rpt(45) }}; {{ rpt("4–5") }}</span></p>
  <p>The oversight committee adopted the final Phase 1 report on {{ ms_by_id["m-2026-05-22-phase1-final"].completed_on | date }}.
    <a href="{{ cfg.report_url }}">Read the Phase 1 report (PDF)</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="commit">
  <h2>2. The agencies commit</h2>
  <p>By July 1, 2026, each agency had to “identify the specific strategies in the final adopted phase one analysis
    that it commits to implementing” and adopt them as formal policy or budget actions.
    <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(b)", 14) }}</span></p>
  <p>The law lets each agency choose which strategies to commit to. All four boards voted before the deadline;
    the <a href="{{ base }}">scorecard</a> shows what each adopted. MTC has since added the adopted strategies to
    its Productivity Improvement Program, with an estimated completion date of June 2029, and agencies report
    progress in their annual funding claims. <span class="rt-cite"><a class="rt-cite-link" href="{{ cfg.pip_url }}">MTC Res. 4743</a></span></p>
  <p><strong>Adopting a strategy is a commitment, not a result.</strong> This tracker records separately whether
    each one is later carried out. See <a href="{{ base }}methodology/#adopted-implemented">adopted versus implemented</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="phase2">
  <h2>3. The independent review, Phase 2 (only if the tax passes)</h2>
  <p>If certified election results show voters approved the tax, Phase 2 must be finished no later than 480 days
    after certification. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(c)", 14) }}</span></p>
  <p>Phase 2 must identify “a menu of cost-saving measures that, if implemented, would reduce one-time and ongoing
    fixed and variable costs,” measured with figures such as cost per passenger mile and subsidy per passenger
    mile. It also includes a regional strategy for developing the agencies' real estate. Both phases must clearly
    separate recommendations that would not affect service from those that would require service changes or cuts.
    <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67762(c)–(d)", 13) }}</span></p>
  {% set cert = ms_by_id["m-election-certified"] %}
  <p class="rt-note">{% if cert.completed_on %}Election results were certified {{ cert.completed_on | date }}.
    {% else %}As of {{ cert.status_as_of | date }}, the election results have not been certified, so the Phase 2
    deadline has no date yet. See the <a href="{{ base }}#m-2026-11-03-election">timeline</a>.{% endif %}</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="plans">
  <h2>4. Implementation plans</h2>
  <ol class="rt-plain-ol">
    <li>Within 120 days after Phase 2 is complete, each agency submits a draft implementation plan describing all
      the cost-saving measures it plans to carry out. The law says the plan must “balance financial efficiency,
      service, and system safety.” <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(d)", 14) }}</span></li>
    <li>The oversight committee approves each draft or recommends revisions. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(e)", 14) }}</span></li>
    <li>Within 60 days of the committee's action, the agency's board adopts a final plan that incorporates the
      committee's recommendations. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(f)", 15) }}</span></li>
    <li>A board may reject a recommendation only by adopting a written finding that it would have “an unacceptable
      impact on transit service or safety” or conflicts with other documented local policies. MTC must notify the
      Legislature within 10 business days. Each finding expires after 12 months unless renewed, and the agency
      must report on it annually. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(f)(3)", 15) }}</span></li>
    <li>Each agency must then follow its final plan until the tax expires, unless circumstances beyond its control
      make that infeasible, in which case it must make a good faith effort. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(g)", 15) }}</span></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="funding">
  <h2>5. Funding depends on compliance</h2>
  <p>To keep receiving money from the tax, each agency must verify with MTC that it is complying with these
    requirements. Before withholding funds, MTC must give the agency 90 days to comply. If the agency still has not
    complied by the time MTC makes its final allocations, the withheld money goes to other public transit in the
    county or counties the agency serves. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(h)", "15–16") }}</span></p>
  <p>MTC, not this tracker, decides whether an agency is in compliance. This site reports what the public record
    shows about each commitment.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="covered">
  <h2>What the tracked recommendations cover</h2>
  <p>The {{ n_total }} Phase 1 recommendations tracked here, by category:</p>
  <ul class="rt-cat-list">
    {% for cat, n in by_category %}<li><span>{{ cat | capitalize }}</span> <strong>{{ n }}</strong></li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p>Examples: studying fare evasion and enforcement, running shorter trains when fewer people ride, leasing
    fiber-optic lines, raising parking revenue, and expanding Clipper BayPass. Each agency page lists every
    recommendation with its sources.</p>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="questions">
  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <dl class="rt-faq">
    <dt>Does “adopted” mean money has been saved?</dt>
    <dd>No. It means the agency's board voted to commit to the strategy. Most Phase 1 strategies are studies or
      plans, and the Phase 1 report does not estimate savings for individual strategies.</dd>
    <dt>Why only these four agencies?</dt>
    <dd>SB 63 names AC Transit, BART, Caltrain, and Muni as the agencies subject to the review. Other agencies that
      receive money from the tax are outside the efficiency review; they are listed on the
      <a href="{{ base }}methodology/#scope">Methodology</a> page. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67762(a)", 12) }}</span></dd>
    <dt>What happens if the tax was not approved?</dt>
    <dd>Phase 2 and the implementation plans are required only if certified results show the tax passed. The
      Phase 1 commitments were due either way. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(b)–(c)", 14) }}</span></dd>
    <dt>Will Phase 2 and the implementation plans be tracked here?</dt>
    <dd>Yes. They will be added when the documents are published, with the same sourcing rules.</dd>
    <dt>Who runs this site?</dt>
    <dd>It is independent and not affiliated with MTC or any agency. See <a href="{{ base }}methodology/#about">who
      maintains this site</a> and the <a href="{{ base }}methodology/#corrections">corrections policy</a>.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="rt-section" id="terms">
  <h2>Terms</h2>
  <dl class="rt-facts">
    <dt>MTC</dt><dd>Metropolitan Transportation Commission, the Bay Area's regional transportation planning and funding agency. It runs the review and allocates the tax revenue.</dd>
    <dt>Oversight committee</dt><dd>The Financial Efficiency Review Independent Oversight Committee described above. It ends once every agency adopts its final implementation plan. <span class="rt-cite">{{ law("67766(g)", 15) }}</span></dd>
    <dt>Subject operators</dt><dd>The law's term for the four agencies under review.</dd>
    <dt>RTM</dt><dd>Regional Transit Measure, the name MTC uses for the tax on the November 2026 ballot.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="rt-meta">Statute citations link to SB 63 (Stats. 2025, Ch. 740); hover for the PDF page. A copy is kept at
    <a href="{{ base }}{{ cfg.statute_local_copy }}">{{ cfg.statute_local_copy }}</a>.</p>
</section>
